"""
MAG 7 COVERED-CALL / BUY-WRITE BACKTESTER
================================================================================
Modeled from the Latest live paper-trading engine's actual strategy rules
(the version with delta-targeted strikes, an 80%-profit-capture exit, a
20%-of-entry-DTE backstop, and cash-only even-split sizing — NOT the older
EOD-flatten-everything version). Every rule below is a direct match to latest
live engine's own logic; see the per-rule notes for exactly which live-engine
method each piece of this file is reproducing.
 
Backtest window: 2022-07-05 -> 2026-09-18
Starting capital: $249,000.00 (lump cash sum available on day 1, 2022-07-05)
Candidate universe: Magnificent Seven only — AAPL, MSFT, GOOGL, AMZN, META,
NVDA, TSLA — matching the live engine's CANDIDATE_UNIVERSE exactly.
 
READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING ANY NUMBER THIS PRINTS:
 
1. IBKR's API does not provide historical bid/ask quotes for expired option
   contracts, so option prices here are NOT real historical quotes — they
   are Black-Scholes model prices using a rolling REALIZED volatility
   computed from each stock's own daily returns (implied vol for expired
   chains isn't available historically either). Real option prices differ
   from BS-modeled prices in ways that matter (skew, term structure,
   supply/demand at that specific strike) — a structural limitation of any
   backtest built this way, not a bug to fix later.
 
2. Strikes are theoretical (the real-valued analytical result of
   solve_strike_for_target_delta, targeting TARGET_DELTA=0.25 — same
   function and same target as the live engine's finalize_target_contract()),
   not real listed strike increments — there's no historical chain to snap
   to, so nothing rounds to the nearest $5 or $10 strike the way a real
   fill would.
 
3. Not modeled at all: commissions, slippage, bid/ask spread on fills,
   dividends (covered calls forgo dividend upside on assignment), and true
   early exercise by the option holder.
 
4. NO END-OF-DAY FLATTEN. This is the key difference from an older version
   of this backtest: the live engine no longer force-closes everything at
   market close. A position, once opened, is held across as many days as it
   takes to hit ONE of three live-engine exits (checked in this exact order,
   matching monitor_exit_targets()/monitor_leg_exit_targets()):
     a) DTE BACKSTOP — remaining days-to-expiry has fallen to
        DTE_BACKSTOP_PCT (20%) of the DTE it had when opened. Force-closes
        regardless of P&L.
     b) PROFIT CAPTURE — the option's modeled value has decayed to
        (1 - PROFIT_CAPTURE_PCT) = 20% of the entry credit.
     c) STOP LOSS — the option's modeled value has grown to
        STOP_LOSS_MULTIPLE (2.0x) the entry credit.
   Positions can and do span multiple trading days, sometimes weeks, exactly
   as they would live.
 
5. RECYCLE, NOT ROLL: whenever a position closes for any reason above, the
   shares are ALSO SOLD, exactly like the live engine's
   recycle_symbol_after_close() — this is not a typical buy-write backtest
   that holds shares through expiration and only sells on assignment. Every
   exit is a full round-trip (stock + option), realizing a fresh stock
   capital gain/loss every cycle, not just option premium.
 
6. CASH-ONLY, EVEN SPLIT: every new buy-write is sized from ACTUAL
   AVAILABLE CASH ONLY — never margin, never more than 100% of cash
   deployed — split evenly across however many symbols still qualify as
   candidates are worked down the ranked list that day, exactly matching
   attempt_new_buy_write()'s `allocation = cash_remaining / candidates_remaining`
   logic (candidates_remaining decrements once per candidate tried,
   including skipped ones, whether or not a trade was actually placed).
 
7. THE DAILY RISK BREAKER FREEZES EVERYTHING, NOT JUST NEW ENTRIES. This is
   a correction versus an earlier version of this backtest. In the live
   code, once max_daily_drawdown_pct (8%) or max_daily_markup_pct (15%) is
   breached, self.system_halted is set True — and tickPrice()/
   tickOptionComputation() both return immediately whenever system_halted
   is True. That means EVERY subsequent market-data callback that day is
   dropped, which freezes not just new position opens but also exit
   monitoring on positions already open — they just sit marked-to-market,
   untouched, until the flag clears. The live engine only ever clears
   system_halted by restarting the process, which in practice happens once
   a day (the engine shuts down at 16:00 and is started fresh each
   morning) — so this backtest treats the breaker as reset once per
   trading day, and on a halted day skips BOTH new entries AND managing
   existing positions, matching that behavior. Because daily bars don't
   give an intraday sequence of events, the breach is checked using each
   day's opening mark (yesterday's close vs. today's close, before any of
   today's management) — the earliest point a daily-bar simulation can
   detect it — rather than mid-day. This is a defensible approximation
   given the data, not a literal event-by-event replay.
 
Use this to sanity-check the STRATEGY RULES the live engine actually runs,
not as a promise of real-world P&L.
"""
 
